它指的是条状的边界。

1129
00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:17.400
Huh?
啊？

1130
00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:18.040
Yeah.
是的。

1131
00:51:18.320 --> 00:51:22.680
For example, a knight participating in a jousting match was said to be
例如，参加比武大赛的骑士被说成是

1132
00:51:22.680 --> 00:51:23.600
in the liSt.
“in the list”（在边界内）。

1133
00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:27.760
That meant he was inside the strip of land marked off for combat.
这意味着他身处为战斗而划定的条形场地之内。

1134
00:51:28.100 --> 00:51:31.500
It also could mean a strip of clothing, a strip of parchment.
它也可以指布条或羊皮纸条。

1135
00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:37.080
But what Shakespeare did here for the very first time using the word list, not to refer
但莎士比亚在这里第一次使用“list”一词，不是指

1136
00:51:37.080 --> 00:51:42.220
to a strip of parchment, but to refer to the words written on the parchment, was the
羊皮纸条，而是指写在纸条上的文字，这是

1137
00:51:42.220 --> 00:51:48.260
first time the word list was used in its modern thinking, which was a writing of series of iteMs.
“list”一词首次以现代含义使用，即书写一系列项目。

1138
00:51:48.700 --> 00:51:52.280
So, Shakespeare did mean a list as we know it today.
所以，莎士比亚确实是指我们今天所理解的“清单”。

1139
00:51:52.480 --> 00:51:52.800
Yes.
是的。

1140
00:51:52.880 --> 00:51:55.200
But at the time, it normally meant border.
但在当时，它通常指边界。

1141
00:51:55.640 --> 00:51:56.080
Yes.
没错。

1142
00:51:57.200 --> 00:51:58.600
He reused the word.
他赋予了这个词新的用法。

1143
00:51:58.860 --> 00:51:59.260
He reused it.
他重新利用了这个词。

1144
00:51:59.640 --> 00:52:05.020
Also, for those of you who are going to say to me that it was lawless resolutes in the original draft that
另外，对于那些想跟我说莎士比亚原稿中写的是“lawless”（无法无天的）的人，

1145
00:52:05.020 --> 00:52:07.300
Shakespeare wrote, it was landless resolutes.
其实是“landless”（无地的）。

1146
00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:12.840
The youngest child who would have had no land, who would have had motivation to go on this adventure.
那是家里最小的孩子，没有土地，因此有动力去参加这场冒险。

1147
00:52:12.840 --> 00:52:16.240
Guys, do not come for Angela on Shakespeare, all right?
