然后帕姆有一个单人采访镜头。

1373
01:02:11.260 --> 01:02:12.880
I remember shooting this.
我记得拍这一幕的时候。

1374
01:02:13.140 --> 01:02:14.720
You're so good in it.
你演得真好。

1375
01:02:14.920 --> 01:02:16.440
You made me tear up.
你让我都看哭了。

1376
01:02:16.960 --> 01:02:19.060
Jim has made his own liSt.
吉姆做了他自己的名单。

1377
01:02:19.220 --> 01:02:22.980
And on one side is Pam, Cece, and the new baby.
一边写着帕姆、西西和刚出生的宝宝。

1378
01:02:23.140 --> 01:02:25.820
And on the other side, it says everything else.
另一边写着其他所有的一切。

1379
01:02:26.480 --> 01:02:27.020
I know.
我知道。

1380
01:02:27.220 --> 01:02:30.160
And then Pam is like, I'm going to frame it.
然后帕姆说，我要把它装裱起来。

1381
01:02:30.740 --> 01:02:32.560
I can always unframe it.
我随时可以把它拆下来。

1382
01:02:32.820 --> 01:02:33.680
I loved that.
我很喜欢那句。

1383
01:02:34.520 --> 01:02:36.440
She's like, am I being overly sentimental?
她说，我是不是太感性了？

1384
01:02:36.440 --> 01:02:37.120
I don't know.
我不知道。

1385
01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:45.400
So we had a fan question from Molly G in Cincinnati who wanted to know, was that line, I'm going to frame it, I can always unframe it, improvised or scripted.
我们收到一个来自辛辛那提的粉丝莫莉·G的问题，她想知道“我要把它装裱起来，我随时可以把它拆下来”这句台词是即兴发挥还是剧本写的。

1386
01:02:45.960 --> 01:02:47.020
It was scripted.
那是剧本写的。

1387
01:02:47.400 --> 01:02:51.740
And Chris C from Madison Heights, Virginia, wanted to know whose handwriting was on the paper.
来自弗吉尼亚州麦迪逊高地的克里斯·C想知道纸上的字是谁写的。

1388
01:02:52.300 --> 01:02:53.420
And it was John's.
是约翰写的。

1389
01:02:53.520 --> 01:02:54.360
He wrote that out.
他写下了那些字。

1390
01:02:54.740 --> 01:02:55.560
That was his handwriting.
那是他的笔迹。

1391
01:02:55.820 --> 01:02:57.080
He has really good handwriting.
他的字写得真好。

1392
01:02:57.360 --> 01:02:57.520
Yeah.
是啊。

1393
01:02:57.700 --> 01:03:01.860
I mean, I think he might have taken some time and care with it, knowing it would be on camera.
我是说，我觉得他可能花了一些时间和心思，因为知道会拍进镜头里。

1394
01:03:02.040 --> 01:03:04.360
But they brought him a piece of paper and they said, write this down.
但他们拿了一张纸给他，让他写下来。

1395
01:03:04.900 --> 01:03:06.500
Well, that was the liSt.
嗯，那就是那份名单。

1396
01:03:06.500 --> 01:03:07.880
We hope you enjoyed it.
希望你们喜欢。

1397
01:03:08.060 --> 01:03:09.400
Are you going to make a list today?
你今天打算列个清单吗？

1398
01:03:09.940 --> 01:03:10.800
Maybe you will.
也许你会的。

1399
01:03:11.180 --> 01:03:13.800
If you do, you can thank William Shakespeare for that.
如果你这么做了，你可以为此感谢威廉·莎士比亚。

1400
01:03:15.540 --> 01:03:18.320
A big thank you to Steve Burgess,
非常感谢史蒂夫·伯吉斯，

1401
01:03:18.500 --> 01:03:25.500
BJ Novak and Paul Lieberstein for letting me reach out to you about this episode and to Kate Flannery for sending in her audio clip about her stunt.
感谢BJ·诺瓦克和保罗·利伯斯坦让我能就这一集联系你们，也感谢凯特·弗兰纳里发来她关于特技表演的音频片段。

1402
01:03:25.500 --> 01:03:29.120
Yes, and to Ellie for answering our voicemail that we left.
是的，还要感谢艾莉回复了我们留下的语音留言。

1403
01:03:29.300 --> 01:03:32.000
You guys, our cast and crew are the absolute beSt.
伙计们，我们的演职人员绝对是最棒的。

1404
01:03:32.420 --> 01:03:35.020
And thanks so much for listening and re-watching with us.
非常感谢你们收听并与我们一起重温剧集。

1405
01:03:35.120 --> 01:03:35.540
We love you.
我们爱你们。

1406
01:03:35.680 --> 01:03:36.460
See you next week.
下周见。

1407
